  "response": "Seismic explanations have been evaluated in the same evidentiary manner, by asking whether the ruin patterns themselves require tectonic agency. Collapsed walls and skeletons buried under debris at Tiryns have been interpreted as earthquake damage, and that pattern of ruin shows what Amos Nur proposed, namely that a sequence of seismic events, termed an earthquake storm, struck the Aegean and Anatolia. Whereas an account of that kind traces the collapse to a single class of mechanism, Eric Cline argued in his book 1177 B.C. that multiple interacting stressors produced the collapse, and the trajectory of the field has run steadily toward such multicausal reconstructions.\n\nClimatic and commercial arguments are assessed by the same standard of convergence. Hittite letters request urgent shipments of grain from Ugarit, which indicates that a severe multi-decade drought affected the Eastern Mediterranean around 1200 BCE. Whatever relative weight is finally assigned to each factor, at least one of two circumstances obtained in those decades \u2014 that drought, or the disruption of the maritime trade routes carrying copper and tin across the Eastern Mediterranean around 1200 BCE \u2014 and perhaps both obtained together. Pollen cores from the Sea of Galilee record a shift to drier conditions in the late thirteenth century BCE, which confirms that a severe multi-decade drought gripped the Eastern Mediterranean around 1200 BCE. The aridification of the Eastern Mediterranean began after 1100 BCE and postdates the urban destruction layers, a sequence that the Galilee pollen record rules out.\n\nAnatolia furnishes a test case in which two reconstructions of imperial history compete for the same ground. Hattusa served as the capital of a continuous Hittite empire until the ninth century BCE. Such continuity cannot be squared with the burning and abandonment of the Hittite capital Hattusa in the early twelfth century BCE, so the choice between the two is forced.\n\nTextual material narrows what the destructions actually swept away, since the loss of bureaucratic writing can be observed directly in the archives rather than inferred from later silence. Linear B administration in the Mycenaean palaces ended with the destructions around 1200 BCE; in other words, the palatial record-keeping system of Mycenaean Greece ceased after the destruction of the palace centers.\n\nAdjudication among rival explanations of the Late Bronze Age collapse proceeds by testing each proposed mechanism against independently dated destruction horizons, administrative archives, and environmental proxies, with preference given not to intuitive plausibility but to the reconstruction that survives contact with all three bodies of material. The Late Bronze Age collapse in the Eastern Mediterranean is dated to roughly 1200\u20131150 BCE, and the destruction by fire of the Mycenaean palace at Pylos around 1180 BCE shows that this chronological bracket is correctly placed.",
